My work lies at the intersection of methodological statistics and applied research. The substantive problem is the starting point; the statistical model follows from the structure of the question and the available data.

## Health, ageing and inequalities

I study morbidity, multimorbidity and health inequalities across population groups, geographical areas and time. A central part of this work concerns repeated cross-sectional surveys and the distinction between genuine population change and changes in the composition of observed samples.

## Environment, health and public perceptions

I investigate relationships between environmental conditions, health outcomes and public awareness. This work includes contextual exposure indicators, territorial differences and the communication of complex evidence to researchers, institutions and communities.

## Statistical modelling

My methodological interests include Bayesian and computational statistics, hierarchical models, state-space models, time series, Monte Carlo methods, survey data and interpretable machine learning. I am particularly interested in models that can represent heterogeneity without losing interpretability.

## Sport and performance

I study longitudinal athletic careers, performance monitoring, talent development and health signals observed through sport data. This research originated during my PhD and continues through collaborations with statisticians and sport scientists.

## Current projects

**PLANET4HEALTH** — Environmental awareness, community perceptions and communication of statistical evidence. Horizon Europe, 2024–present.

**Age-It, Spoke 10** — Local and temporal dynamics of morbidity and health inequalities from repeated cross-sectional surveys. 2023–present.

**Measuring Well-Being** — Analysis of national well-being frameworks and their connections with environmental dimensions. WHO, 2024–present.
